### `setup-db.sh`

```bash
#!/bin/bash

set -e

echo "======================================"
echo " Database Setup"
echo "======================================"

# Check Supabase CLI
if ! command -v supabase &> /dev/null
then
    echo "Supabase CLI is not installed."
    echo "Please install it first:"
    echo "https://supabase.com/docs/guides/cli"
    exit 1
fi

echo ""
echo "Starting Supabase..."
supabase start

echo ""
echo "Applying database migrations..."
supabase db reset

echo ""
echo "======================================"
echo " Database setup completed successfully"
echo "======================================"
```

### Run it

From the project root:

```bash
chmod +x setup-db.sh
./setup-db.sh
```

Or:

```bash
bash setup-db.sh
```

The script expects your migrations to be stored here:

```text
supabase/
└── migrations/
    ├── 0001_backend_jwt_auth.sql
    ├── 0002_disable_rls.sql
    ├── 0003_roles_expenses_driver_tasks.sql
    ├── 0004_reviews.sql
    └── 0005_schema_consistency.sql
```

`supabase db reset` will recreate the local database and execute the migrations in order.

### Recommended repository structure

```text
project/
├── setup-db.sh
├── package.json
├── .env
└── supabase/
    └── migrations/
        ├── 0001_backend_jwt_auth.sql
        ├── 0002_disable_rls.sql
        ├── 0003_roles_expenses_driver_tasks.sql
        ├── 0004_reviews.sql
        └── 0005_schema_consistency.sql
```

For a completely new laptop, the setup becomes:

```bash
git clone <repository-url>
cd <project-folder>

chmod +x setup-db.sh
./setup-db.sh
```

This gives you a **single setup script** that starts Supabase and recreates the database from the version-controlled migrations.
